A stack gives the values in reverse order. The first walk pushes every node value, and the second walk compares the list from the front with values popped from the stack.

The list itself is not changed. Empty and one-node lists also work because they have no pair of different values to compare.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

/* Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            stack.push(current.val);
            current = current.next;
        }

        current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.val != stack.pop()) {
                return false;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack.

The slow and fast pointers find the start of the second half. That half is reversed, then the two halves are compared node by node. The second half is reversed again before returning, so the input list is restored.

Dry run for `1 -> 2 -> 2 -> 1`:
- The second half starts at the second `2`.
- Reversing it gives `1 -> 2`.
- The first half `1 -> 2` matches it, so the list is a palindrome.

```cpp
/*
Definition for singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
private:
    ListNode* reverseList(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode* previous = NULL;
        ListNode* current = head;

        while (current != NULL) {
            ListNode* nextNode = current->next;
            current->next = previous;
            previous = current;
            current = nextNode;
        }

        return previous;
    }

public:
    bool isPalindrome(ListNode* head) {
        if (head == NULL || head->next == NULL) {
            return true;
        }

        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;
        }

        ListNode* secondHalf = reverseList(slow);
        ListNode* savedSecondHalf = secondHalf;
        ListNode* firstHalf = head;
        bool result = true;

        while (secondHalf != NULL) {
            if (firstHalf->val != secondHalf->val) {
                result = false;
                break;
            }
            firstHalf = firstHalf->next;
            secondHalf = secondHalf->next;
        }

        reverseList(savedSecondHalf);
        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The links are changed briefly, then restored.

All node values go into a deque. Removing one value from the front and one from the back checks the matching pair directly. A mismatch stops the check early.

For example, with `4 -> 7 -> 9 -> 7 -> 4`, the pairs checked are `(4, 4)` and `(7, 7)`. The middle `9` does not need a partner.

The original links are left untouched, which makes this a simple option when extra memory is acceptable.

