        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

#include <deque>

class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(ListNode* head) {
        std::deque<int> values;
        ListNode* current = head;

        while (current != NULL) {
            values.push_back(current->val);
            current = current->next;
        }

        while (values.size() > 1) {
            if (values.front() != values.back()) {
                return false;
            }
            values.pop_front();
            values.pop_back();
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the deque.

Recursion reaches the end of the list first. While the calls return, each node is compared with a pointer that moves forward from the head. This compares matching positions from the two ends without storing all values.

Important edge cases are a `nil` head and a single node. Both are palindromes and return `true` immediately through the base condition.

```go
/*
Definition for singly-linked list.
type ListNode struct {
    Val  int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func isPalindrome(head *ListNode) bool {
    left := head

    var check func(*ListNode) bool
    check = func(node *ListNode) bool {
        if node == nil {
            return true
        }

        if !check(node.Next) {
            return false
        }

        if left.Val != node.Val {
            return false
        }

        left = left.Next
        return true
    }

    return check(head)
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the recursive call stack.

